    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures). The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required: when more than 80 owners have already been created today, include `bulkSignupWarning: true` in the owner response; otherwise `false`.\n\nI followed the existing convention for create-time-derived, persisted owner fields (like `customerCode`, `namesakeCount`, `membershipNumber`):\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `bulkSignupWarning` boolean property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** (entity) \u2014 added the `bulkSignupWarning` column with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 set `owner.setBulkSignupWarning(ownersCreatedThatDay > 80)` before saving. This reuses the existing `ownersCreatedThatDay` count (owners already created for that registration date), the same accumulation path as the daily-limit rule \u2014 so \"more than 80 already created\" \u21d2 `> 80` \u21d2 warning true.\n\n4. **schema.sql** (hsqldb / h2 / mysql / postgres) \u2014 added the `bulk_signup_warning` column so persistence works across all DB profiles.\n\nMapStruct auto-maps the matching `bulkSignupWarning` field, so it appears both in the `POST` create response and when read back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`, reflecting the state at creation time. The acceptance test's `false` branch passes; the `>80` branch produces `true` via the shared count.",
